-- Residue classes: a change of representation, with the record keeping
-- the difference between what it verified and what it was told.
--
-- Two textbook exercises. First: the square of any integer leaves a
-- remainder of 0 or 1 when divided by 4. Second: in a Pythagorean
-- triple, a² + b² = c², the two legs a and b cannot both be odd.
--
-- Both are statements about all integers, and there are infinitely
-- many of those. The trick that makes them easy is to stop looking at
-- integers and look at remainders instead. Divide any integer by 4 and
-- the remainder is 0, 1, 2 or 3: four RESIDUES. Squaring and adding
-- respect that reduction, so a question about every integer becomes a
-- question about four residues, and four residues can be checked one by
-- one. Square each: 0, 1, 0, 1. So a square is 0 or 1 mod 4, and two
-- odd squares add to 1 + 1 = 2, which no square is. Both exercises fall
-- out of one table of four rows.
--
-- That is the shape of a great deal of hard reasoning: change the
-- representation, and a hard problem becomes a finite one. This
-- programme governs such a change. The point is not the arithmetic; it
-- is keeping three different kinds of standing apart, so that the
-- record never says more than it can back:
--
--   * what the record VERIFIED. The finite statements over the residues
--     are rules here, checked exhaustively over a domain the record
--     has confirmed is complete. The explorer proposes the table; the
--     record recomputes every entry and refuses any statement the
--     table does not bear out. Nothing is taken on trust.
--   * what the record was TOLD. The theorem about every integer is
--     beyond any rule here: there are infinitely many integers. An
--     outside checker (a proof assistant) proves it whole, and reports
--     under its own name, which version it ran, and the exact file it
--     checked. The record keeps who said so about what. It never says
--     "proved", and it never re-runs the checker.
--   * what STANDS. A conclusion about all integers is admitted only
--     when a checker has checked it and the record has verified, for
--     itself, the finite core this programme pairs it with. The pairing
--     is written in the rules: the programme names the two conclusions
--     it recognises and the finite statement each rests on. A conclusion
--     it does not name cannot be admitted, whatever a checker says of
--     it. A read shows which conclusions still stand on a check nobody
--     has withdrawn.
--
-- Three words that recur below. The runtime stores CLAIMS, statements
-- it has agreed to admit because they obey the rules, never "facts". An
-- INVARIANT is a rule the runtime will not let any change break. A
-- `require` is a gate on one act: a yes-or-no question asked before it
-- may go ahead.

program residue_classes

-- The finite domain: the residues of one modulus. The explorer proposes
-- them as one act, and the rules below decide whether they are the
-- whole domain. Append only: a domain is never edited once declared,
-- so nothing verified over it can lose its ground.
predicate Residue(modulus: Decimal, r: Decimal)
    append only
predicate DomainSealed(modulus: Decimal)
    append only

-- The explorer's representation of squaring: one row per residue. The
-- row's square is the explorer's figure, and the record recomputes it.
predicate SquareOf(modulus: Decimal, r: Decimal, square: Decimal)
    unique by (modulus, r)
    append only

-- A finite statement the record has verified over a modulus. The
-- statement is named by a constant the rules below know; the record
-- admits this claim only when its own rule for that statement holds
-- over a complete domain with every square tabulated.
predicate Verified(statement: Subject, modulus: Decimal)
    append only

-- Who may say a theorem was checked. The operator is the trust root and
-- appoints checkers; a checker's name is bound to one database login,
-- so a proposal naming it from any other login is refused before the
-- body runs. The two policy claims are the runtime's own shape for
-- that binding.
predicate Operator(operator: Subject)
predicate Checker(checker: Subject)
predicate ActorAssertionRestricted(actor: Subject)
predicate ActorAssertionAuthority(actor: Subject, login_role: Subject)

-- A checker's report: this statement checks, under this version of the
-- checker, in this exact file (named by its digest). The statement is
-- the theorem's own name in that file. Testimony with a name on it,
-- kept whole. A later finding that the check was wrong is a new claim
-- beside it; the report itself is never erased.
predicate Checked(statement: Subject, checker: Subject, version: Subject, artefact: Subject)
    append only
predicate Withdrawn(statement: Subject, checker: Subject, reason: Subject)
    append only

-- A conclusion about all integers, paired with the finite statement
-- that is its core. The conclusion itself is what the checker checked;
-- the core is what this record verified.
predicate Conclusion(conclusion: Subject, statement: Subject)
    unique by (conclusion)
    append only

-- The domain. A residue is an integer in [0, modulus), and a sealed
-- domain of a positive modulus holds the modulus's worth of them. Since
-- claims are a set and each one is an integer in range, that count
-- means every residue is present: the domain is complete, not merely
-- plausible.
invariant residue_is_an_integer_in_range:
    Residue(m, r) implies 0 <= r and r < m and r % 1 = 0

invariant sealed_domain_is_complete:
    DomainSealed(m) implies 0 < m and sum(1 | Residue(m, _)) = m

-- The table. Every square is the record's own recompute.
invariant square_is_the_recompute:
    SquareOf(m, r, s) implies s = (r * r) % m

-- What "verified" costs. A statement is checked only over a sealed
-- domain with every residue's square in the table. Without these two
-- rules, a statement could pass over a table with the awkward rows
-- left out.
invariant verified_domain_is_sealed:
    Verified(_, m) implies DomainSealed(m)

invariant verified_squares_are_total:
    Verified(_, m) and Residue(m, r) implies SquareOf(m, r, _)

-- The first exercise, over residues: every square in the table is 0 or
-- 1. Propose the same statement for modulus 8 and the row for 2 (its
-- square is 4) refuses it, and the refusal names that row.
invariant squares_are_zero_or_one:
    Verified(#squares_are_zero_or_one, m) and SquareOf(m, _, s)
    implies s = 0 or s = 1

-- The second exercise, over residues: no square in the table equals the
-- sum of two odd squares. Every pair of odd residues is tried against
-- every square.
invariant no_square_is_a_sum_of_two_odd_squares:
    (Verified(#no_square_is_a_sum_of_two_odd_squares, m)
     and Residue(m, a) and Residue(m, b) and a % 2 = 1 and b % 2 = 1
     and SquareOf(m, _, s))
    implies s != (a * a + b * b) % m

-- Only a statement this programme has a rule for can be verified. A
-- name the rules do not know would otherwise be admitted unchecked.
invariant only_a_stated_statement_is_verified:
    Verified(statement, modulus)
    implies (statement = #squares_are_zero_or_one
             or statement = #no_square_is_a_sum_of_two_odd_squares)

-- What a conclusion needs. First, the pairing: this programme knows two
-- theorems, each resting on one finite statement over the residues
-- modulo 4, and admits no other conclusion. A checker's report about
-- some other statement is kept as a report and grounds nothing here.
-- Then the two grounds: the core verified by this record, and the
-- conclusion checked by a checker.
invariant conclusion_rests_on_its_own_finite_core:
    Conclusion(conclusion, statement)
    implies ((conclusion = #every_square_is_zero_or_one_mod_four
              and statement = #squares_are_zero_or_one)
             or (conclusion = #no_pythagorean_triple_has_two_odd_legs
                 and statement = #no_square_is_a_sum_of_two_odd_squares))

invariant conclusion_rests_on_a_verified_core:
    Conclusion(_, statement) implies Verified(statement, 4)

invariant conclusion_rests_on_a_check:
    Conclusion(conclusion, _) implies Checked(statement: conclusion, ..)

-- The acts.

-- The deployment's first act names the operator and binds the name to
-- its login in the same breath, so nobody else can ever speak it.
transformation appoint_operator(operator, login_role):
    require not Operator(_)
    admit Operator(operator)
    admit ActorAssertionRestricted(operator)
    admit ActorAssertionAuthority(operator, login_role)

transformation appoint_checker(checker, login_role):
    require Operator(actor)
    require not Checker(checker)
    admit Checker(checker)
    admit ActorAssertionRestricted(checker)
    admit ActorAssertionAuthority(checker, login_role)

-- The explorer's acts. Anyone may propose a domain, a row, a statement
-- or a conclusion; the rules decide.
transformation declare_domain(modulus, residues):
    require not DomainSealed(modulus)
    for r in residues:
        admit Residue(modulus, r)
    admit DomainSealed(modulus)

-- A row is for a residue the domain declares; the rule above decides
-- whether its square is right.
transformation tabulate_square(modulus, r, square):
    require Residue(modulus, r)
    admit SquareOf(modulus, r, square)

transformation verify(statement, modulus):
    admit Verified(statement, modulus)

transformation conclude(conclusion, statement):
    admit Conclusion(conclusion, statement)

-- The checker's acts, under its own bound name.
transformation record_check(statement, version, artefact):
    require Checker(actor)
    admit Checked(statement, actor, version, artefact)

transformation withdraw_check(statement, reason):
    require Checked(statement, actor, _, _)
    admit Withdrawn(statement, actor, reason)

-- The read: which conclusions stand, on whose word, and how many of that
-- checker's reports back each. Computed from the claims whenever asked,
-- never stored, so it cannot drift from them. A conclusion drops out
-- the moment its checker withdraws, while every claim that put it there
-- stays on the record.
predicate Standing(conclusion: Subject, statement: Subject, checker: Subject, checks: Decimal)

derived Standing(conclusion, statement, checker):
    over (Conclusion(conclusion, statement)
          and Checked(conclusion, checker, _, _)
          and not Withdrawn(conclusion, checker, _))
    value checks = sum(1 | Checked(conclusion, checker, _, _))
